**Evidence Synthesis for Cotrimoxazole Prophylaxis in HIV-Infected Children**                                           
                                                                                                                        
One randomised controlled trial (CHAP Trial) was identified that directly addresses the research question regarding     
cotrimoxazole prophylaxis in children with HIV infection [1].                                                           
                                                                                                                        
**Primary Outcome: All-Cause Mortality**                                                                                
                                                                                                                        
Bwakura-Dangawa et al. (2003) conducted a double-blind, randomised, placebo-controlled trial in Zambia involving 541    
HIV-infected children aged 1-14 years, with 534 children included in the final analysis [1]. The trial demonstrated a   
significant reduction in all-cause mortality with cotrimoxazole prophylaxis compared to placebo. The hazard ratio for   
mortality was 0.57 (95% CI 0.43-0.77, p=0.0002), representing a 43% reduction in mortality risk [1]. Specifically, 74   
children (28%) died in the cotrimoxazole group versus 112 children (42%) in the placebo group [1].                      
                                                                                                                        
The mortality benefit was consistent across all age groups (interaction p=0.82), baseline CD4 counts (interaction       
p=0.36), and persisted beyond 12 months of follow-up where HR was 0.48 (95% CI 0.27-0.84) among 320 children followed   
for extended periods [1].                                                                                               
                                                                                                                        
**Secondary Outcomes and Safety**                                                                                       
                                                                                                                        
Regarding adverse events, there was no significant difference in grade 3 or 4 adverse events between cotrimoxazole and  
placebo groups (16 children, 6% vs 18 children, 7%) [1]. Specifically, neutrophil count <0.5x10^9/L was observed in 6%  
of children receiving cotrimoxazole versus 3% in placebo (p=0.06), which did not reach statistical significance [1].    
Only one case of rash was reported in the placebo group [1].                                                            
                                                                                                                        
**Clinical Implications and Trial Characteristics**                                                                     
                                                                                                                        
The CHAP trial was conducted in a resource-poor Zambian setting with high levels of bacterial resistance to             
cotrimoxazole, demonstrating the robustness of the intervention even in resistant environments [1]. The data and safety 
monitoring committee stopped the trial early in October 2003 due to clear mortality benefit [1]. Notably, Pneumocystis  
carinii was identified in only 1 out of 73 nasopharyngeal aspirates from children with pneumonia in the placebo group,  
suggesting pneumonia prevention may not be the primary mechanism of benefit [1].                                        
                                                                                                                        
**Evidence Strength and Limitations**                                                                                   
                                                                                                                        
The evidence is based on a single high-quality RCT with low risk of bias, but generalizability may be limited to similar
resource-poor settings. The early termination of the trial raises potential concerns about effect size overestimation.  
Further research is needed to assess very long-term safety and efficacy in diverse settings.                            
                                                                                                                        
**Conclusion**                                                                                                          
                                                                                                                        
The available evidence from one randomised controlled trial demonstrates that daily cotrimoxazole prophylaxis           
significantly reduces mortality in children aged 1-14 years with clinical features of HIV infection in resource-poor    
settings, with no significant increase in serious adverse events [1]. The evidence strength is moderate given the single
study source and early trial termination, despite the study's methodological quality."""                                
                                                                                                                        
